  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">China applies the international labour standards in national laws and policies. China&#x2019;s laws and regulations prohibit child labour, forced labour, employment discrimination and sexual harassment in the workplace, and promote occupational safety and health at work. However, there are gaps remaining in laws and enforcement comparing with international labour Conventions. Labour laws have not covered workers who are not in an employment relationship and contract. Further policy reform and actions should be taken for improving the national legal system in line with international labour standards. 
To reaffirm China&#x2019;s commitment to prevention and abolition of forced labour, the Standing Committee of the National People&apos;s Congress voted for ratification of the Forced Labour Convention, 1930 (No. 29) and the Abolition of Forced Labour Convention, 1957 (No. 105), and deposited the ratification of these two instruments with the ILO on 12 August 2022. With ratification of the forced labour Conventions (C.29 and C105), effective application of the forced labour standards becomes particularly important. 
The Country Outcome Programme CHN826 aim to strengthen constituents&#x2019; capacity to ratify international labour standards (ILS), enhance constituents&#x2019; knowledge and understanding of international labour standards, and improve quality of reporting. ILO technical assistance helps constituents to apply ILS as a crucial mean for governing social and economic development in an era of dynamic changes, and addressing problems arising from application of ILS at national level. 

The ILO interventions in this biennium will focus on capacity building for constituents and line ministries on application of C. 29 and C105,  providing continuous support for tripartite constituents to improve national actions and practices for application of ratified Conventions in line with comments and suggestions of Committee of Experts on the Application of Conventions and Recommendations (CEACR).</narrative>
  </description>
